extract_html = """<p><b><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">Sodium</a></b> (symbol <b>Na</b>, atomic number 11) is a remarkably soft, silvery-white, and highly reactive alkali metal in Group 1 of the periodic table. Soft enough to cut with a butter knife, free sodium metal is so chemically energetic that it never exists naturally on Earth; it must be manufactured through electrolysis from its mineral salts. As the sixth most abundant element in Earth's crust, sodium makes up about 2.27% of planet Earth, found across minerals like feldspars, sodalite, and halite (common rock salt, NaCl). Over eons of geological time, liquid water has leached soluble sodium ions from surface rocks, carrying them into rivers and oceans. Today, sodium ions and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a> ions reign as the most abundant dissolved minerals by weight in world oceans. Featuring a single stable isotope (<sup>23</sup>Na), sodium powers human nerve signals, illuminates night highways with golden streetlamps, cools advanced nuclear reactors, and drives next-generation sodium-ion energy storage batteries.</p>"""

history_html = """<p>Because common salt (<a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> chloride) is an absolute necessity for human survival, sodium compounds have been cherished commodities since the dawn of civilization. In medieval Europe, an alkaline sodium compound known as <i>sodanum</i> was widely prescribed as a soothing headache remedy. The word <i>sodium</i> itself traces back to the Arabic word <i>suda</i>, meaning "headache," reflecting ancient medicinal recognition of soda's pain-alleviating qualities.</p>
<p>In ancient India, sodium chemistry flourished through the practice of Ayurveda and <i>Rasaśāstra</i> (iatrochemistry). Classical treatises like the <i>Suśruta Saṁhitā</i> and <i>Rasataraṅgiṇī</i> describe an essential plant-derived alkaline medicine called <i>Swarjikā Kṣāra</i> (also known as <i>Sajjikṣāra</i> or <i>Sarjika Kshara</i>). Ancient Indian physicians prepared this potent alkali by burning halophytic (salt-loving) desert plants like <i>Fagonia cretica</i> (Ushtrapriya) and <i>Cressa cretica</i> (Rudanti). Chemically dominated by sodium carbonate (Na₂CO₃) and sodium bicarbonate (NaHCO₃), <i>Sajjikṣāra</i> was classified as a vital <i>Pānīya Kṣāra</i> (internal alkaline medicine) used to soothe hyperacidity, dissolve digestive blockages (<i>Gulma</i>), and treat abdominal colic and skin conditions. Ancient texts like the <i>Caraka Saṁhitā</i> also carefully categorized <i>Saindhava Lavaṇa</i> (pure mineral rock salt) and <i>Samudra Lavaṇa</i> (sea salt) for their therapeutic, preservation, and culinary properties.</p>
<p>In global scientific history, although sodium compounds like soda ash and saltpetre were used for centuries, pure metallic sodium remained hidden because of its intense chemical reactivity. In 1807, legendary English chemist Sir Humphry Davy achieved a historic breakthrough by passing an electric current through molten sodium hydroxide (caustic soda)—a process called electrolysis—to isolate tiny, gleaming spheres of pure sodium metal for the very first time. In 1809, German chemist Ludwig Wilhelm Gilbert proposed the name <i>Natronium</i> for Davy's new metal. Later in 1814, Swedish chemist Jöns Jakob Berzelius selected the official chemical symbol <b>Na</b> from the Neo-Latin name <i>natrium</i>, derived from <i>natron</i>—the natural hydrated sodium carbonate salt mined by ancient Egyptians for mummification, cleansing, and glassmaking.</p>
<p>Sodium played a legendary role in the birth of modern spectroscopy. In 1860, pioneering German scientists Gustav Kirchhoff and Robert Bunsen discovered that burning sodium compounds imparts a bright, unmistakable yellow light to flames. In a famous demonstration published in <i>Annalen der Physik und Chemie</i>, Kirchhoff and Bunsen detonated just 3 milligrams of sodium chlorate in the far corner of a huge 60-cubic-meter lecture hall. The sodium flame test proved so sensitive that it instantly lit up the room's spectroscope, revealing that scientists could detect as little as 1/20-millionth of a milligram of sodium in a single unit of air!</p>
<p>In 20th-century history, common sodium chloride became a world-changing symbol of freedom. On March 12, 1930, Mahatma Gandhi launched the historic 388-kilometer Dandi Salt March to coastal Gujarat. By picking up a pinch of natural sea salt on the shores of Dandi, Gandhi broke the tyrannical British salt monopoly, transforming simple sodium chloride into the moral spark for India's independence movement.</p>"""

characteristics_html = """<p>Pure metallic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> is a soft, bright, silvery metal with a low melting point of 370.944 K (97.79 °C / 208.02 °F) and a boiling point of 1156.09 K (882.94 °C / 1621.3 °F). With a density of only 0.968 g/cm³, pure sodium is actually lighter than water! If placed on water, a piece of sodium floats—though not for long, because it reacts explosively! Sodium is so soft at room temperature that it can easily be cut with a butter knife or squeezed into ribbons. When freshly cut, it exhibits a brilliant metallic sheen, but within seconds of exposure to moist air, it rapidly tarnishes into a dull grey coat of sodium oxide, sodium hydroxide, and sodium carbonate.</p>
<p>Chemically, sodium is a quintessential alkali metal sitting in Group 1 with an electron configuration of [Ne] 3s¹ (or 1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹). Holding a single, loosely bound valence electron in its outermost shell, sodium has a low Pauling electronegativity of 0.93 and a low first ionization energy of 495.8 kJ/mol. Because it eagerly sheds this outer electron to achieve the ultra-stable closed-shell configuration of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ne" class="wiki-link" title="Neon (Ne)">neon</a>, sodium forms the single-charged sodium cation (Na⁺) in nearly all its compounds. Sodium reacts violently with liquid water, releasing flammable <a href="/school1/ptable/element/H" class="wiki-link" title="Hydrogen (H)">hydrogen</a> gas and forming caustic sodium hydroxide (2 Na + 2 H₂O → 2 NaOH + H₂↑). The intense heat generated by this reaction frequently ignites the escaping hydrogen gas, producing a dramatic, fiery yellow flame.</p>
<p>Sodium metal also exhibits fascinating behavior when dissolved in non-water solvents. Dissolved in liquid ammonia, sodium loses its valence electrons into the solution to form solvated electrons, producing an extraordinary deep-blue conducting liquid that acts as a powerful reducing agent in organic chemistry. Under extremely dry conditions, when metallic sodium is reacted with benzophenone in organic solvents like tetrahydrofuran, it generates an intense indigo-blue ketyl radical color that serves as a visual indicator confirming the total absence of moisture.</p>"""

isotopes_html = """<p>Nuclear physicists have identified 20 known isotopes of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a>, ranging from <sup>18</sup>Na to <sup>37</sup>Na. However, virtually all sodium found on Earth and across the universe consists of a single stable, primordial isotope: sodium-23 (<sup>23</sup>Na). In deep space, <sup>23</sup>Na is forged inside massive stars (at least 3 solar masses) during stellar carbon-burning. When core temperatures reach a searing 600 megakelvins (600 million degrees Celsius), two <a href="/school1/ptable/element/C" class="wiki-link" title="Carbon (C)">carbon</a> nuclei fuse together to produce sodium-23 along with a proton.</p>
<p>All other sodium isotopes are radioactive with relatively short half-lives. The two most notable naturally occurring radioactive isotopes are cosmogenic nucleomaterials created when high-energy cosmic rays strike atmospheric atoms: sodium-22 (<sup>22</sup>Na) with a half-life of 2.602 years, and sodium-24 (<sup>24</sup>Na) with a half-life of 14.96 hours. All other radioisotopes of sodium have half-lives of less than one single minute! Scientists have also discovered two metastable nuclear isomers, the longest-lived being <sup>24m</sup>Na with a fleeting half-life of 20.2 milliseconds.</p>
<p>Sodium isotopes play a crucial role in emergency nuclear medicine and radiation safety. If a person is exposed to acute neutron radiation—such as during a nuclear criticality accident—thermal neutrons strike the stable <sup>23</sup>Na atoms flowing in human blood plasma, converting some of them into radioactive <sup>24</sup>Na through neutron activation (<sup>23</sup>Na + n → <sup>24</sup>Na). By measuring the precise concentration of <sup>24</sup>Na relative to <sup>23</sup>Na in a patient's blood sample, medical physicists can calculate the exact absorbed dose of neutron radiation received by the victim, enabling rapid life-saving medical treatment.</p>"""

occurrence_html = """<p>Making up 2.27% of Earth's crust, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> is the sixth most abundant element in the crust and the fourth most abundant metal—ranking behind <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Al" class="wiki-link" title="Aluminum (Al)">aluminum</a>, <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Fe" class="wiki-link" title="Iron (Fe)">iron</a>, and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ca" class="wiki-link" title="Calcium (Ca)">calcium</a>, and just ahead of <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a>. Across the wider cosmos, sodium ranks as the 15th most abundant element by mass, accounting for roughly 0.002% of all universal atoms (20,000 parts per billion). Because pure sodium metal reacts instantly with air and water, it is never found uncombined in nature.</p>
<p>Instead, sodium occurs in a wide variety of minerals. Highly soluble mineral salts include halite (rock salt, NaCl) and natron (hydrated sodium carbonate). Less soluble minerals include silicate minerals like amphibole and zeolites, while completely insoluble sodium minerals include cryolite (Na₃AlF₆) and sodium feldspars (such as albite, NaAlSi₃O₈), whose insolubility stems from their complex, interconnected polysilicate polymeric anion networks.</p>
<p>Because many sodium salts dissolve readily in liquid water, rainfall has washed sodium ions from continental rocks into surface rivers and oceans over billions of years. Ocean water contains an impressive average sodium concentration of 10.8 grams per liter (about 1.08% by weight), making sodium and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a> the primary dissolved ions in seawater.</p>
<p>In India, geographical sites showcase immense natural sodium wealth. Rajasthan's iconic <b>Sambhar Salt Lake</b>—India's largest inland saltwater lake—is a vital ecological Ramsar wetland and a historic inland salt production basin. Mentioned in ancient epics like the <i>Mahābhārata</i> (associated with King Vrishparva) and revered as the domain of Goddess Shakambhari of the Chauhan Rajputs, Sambhar Lake yields thousands of tonnes of sodium chloride and sodium sulfate annually. Furthermore, India's extensive salt pans across coastal Gujarat (the vast saline marshlands of the Rann of Kutch) and Tuticorin in Tamil Nadu make India the third-largest producer of salt in the world.</p>"""

applications_html = """<p>While free metallic <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">sodium</a> has specialized industrial roles, the vast majority of sodium is consumed in the form of its chemical compounds. Tens of millions of tonnes of sodium salts are produced globally each year for heavy industry, food, and manufacturing:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Sodium Chloride (NaCl):</b> Common table salt is vital for food preservation, culinary seasoning, and winter road anti-icing. It serves as the primary chemical feedstock for the chlor-alkali industry to produce chlorine gas and sodium hydroxide.</li>
  <li><b>Sodium Carbonate (Na₂CO₃):</b> Known as soda ash, it is essential for glass manufacturing, detergent formulations, and water softening.</li>
  <li><b>Sodium Bicarbonate (NaHCO₃):</b> Popularly known as baking soda, it acts as a leavening agent in cooking, an antacid, and a mild abrasive in sodablasting.</li>
  <li><b>Sodium Hydroxide (NaOH):</b> Known as caustic soda, it is a key industrial base used in soap making, paper pulp production, textile treatment, and chemical drain cleaners.</li>
</ul>
<p>In pharmaceuticals, many active drugs are formulated as sodium salts—such as sodium naproxen, diclofenac sodium, and sodium ampicillin—because sodium ions dramatically enhance water solubility and drug bioavailability in the human body at a far lower cost and lighter atomic weight than <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a>.</p>
<p>Pure metallic sodium is utilized industrially to synthesize specialized chemical compounds like sodium borohydride (NaBH₄, a powerful reducing agent), sodium azide (NaN₃, used in automobile airbags), indigo dye for blue jeans, and triphenylphosphine. Historically, vast quantities of sodium metal were used to manufacture tetraethyllead (an anti-knock gasoline additive) and isolate pure <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Ti" class="wiki-link" title="Titanium (Ti)">titanium</a> metal via the Hunter process; though production declined after 1970 as leaded gasoline was phased out worldwide. In organic laboratory synthesis, sodium metal drives famous organic reactions such as the Birch reduction, the sodium fusion test for qualitative element analysis, and the preparation of sodium alkoxides from alcohols. Dissolved in liquid ammonia, sodium reduces alkynes to <i>trans</i>-alkenes.</p>
<p>In modern high-tech engineering and optics, sodium plays astounding roles. High-pressure and low-pressure sodium vapor lamps emit an efficient yellow-orange to peach-amber glow, illuminating city streets and highways worldwide. In astronomy, ground-based observatories shine powerful yellow lasers tuned to the sodium D-line (589 nm) into the upper atmosphere (~90 km altitude), exciting mesospheric sodium atoms to create an "artificial laser guide star." This technique allows adaptive optics systems to compensate for atmospheric turbulence, producing ultra-sharp space imagery.</p>
<p><b>Modern Indian Scientific Contributions:</b></p>
<p>India is a global leader in utilizing sodium for nuclear power, advanced energy storage, and public health science:</p>
<ul>
  <li><b>Nuclear Energy (IGCAR Kalpakkam):</b> At the Indira Gandhi Centre for Atomic Research (IGCAR) in Kalpakkam, Tamil Nadu, Indian nuclear scientists have mastered the engineering of high-temperature liquid sodium metal as a coolant for Fast Breeder Reactors. Used in the Fast Breeder Test Reactor (FBTR) and the 500 MWe Prototype Fast Breeder Reactor (PFBR), liquid sodium offers superior thermal conductivity and low neutron absorption. IGCAR scientists engineered specialized electromagnetic sodium pumps, safety rigs like SADHANA and SOWART, and under-sodium ultrasonic vision systems to safely navigate high-temperature liquid sodium pools.</li>
  <li><b>Sodium-Ion Battery Tech (CSIR-CECRI):</b> To drive India's clean energy transition and achieve self-reliance (<i>Ātmanirbhar Bhārat</i>), researchers at the CSIR-Central Electrochemical Research Institute (CECRI) in Karaikudi, alongside premier IITs and IISc, are pioneering indigenous <b>Sodium-Ion Batteries (SIBs)</b>. Utilizing abundant local sodium resources, CECRI has fabricated 18650 cylindrical cells using NASICON-type cathode materials and hard carbon anodes, delivering a sustainable, affordable, and safe alternative to imported lithium-ion batteries for electric vehicles and power grids.</li>
  <li><b>Food Science & Public Health (CSIR-CFTRI):</b> Scientists at the CSIR-Central Food Technological Research Institute (CFTRI) in Mysuru conduct cutting-edge food technology research focused on "sodium reduction" in traditional Indian packaged foods, striking a scientific balance between reducing dietary sodium to combat hypertension and preserving authentic traditional flavors.</li>
</ul>"""

biological_role_html = """<p><a href="/school1/ptable/element/Na" class="wiki-link" title="Sodium (Na)">Sodium</a> is an essential mineral nutrient and the dominant positively charged ion (cation) in the extracellular fluids of all animals, including humans. Together with <a href="/school1/ptable/element/Cl" class="wiki-link" title="Chlorine (Cl)">chlorine</a> ions (Cl⁻) and <a href="/school1/ptable/element/K" class="wiki-link" title="Potassium (K)">potassium</a> ions (K⁺), sodium maintains osmotic pressure, regulates fluid balance across cell membranes, and controls blood volume and blood pressure.</p>
<p>At the cellular level, sodium ions are vital for nerve signal transmission and muscle contraction. Cells utilize a specialized membrane-bound protein enzyme called the sodium-potassium pump (Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase) to actively pump three sodium ions out of the cell for every two potassium ions pumped in. This process generates an electrical potential gradient across cell membranes. When a nerve cell fires, sodium channels open instantly, allowing Na⁺ ions to rush into the cell and create an electrical action potential that travels rapidly down nerve pathways to command heartbeats, muscle movements, and brain thoughts.</p>
<p>While most plants require very little sodium (unlike potassium), sodium is an essential micronutrient for C4 plants—such as sugarcane, maize (corn), and salt-tolerant desert halophytes—which use sodium ions to regenerate phosphoenolpyruvate during specialized carbon fixation.</p>
<p>In human health, maintaining the right sodium balance is critical. While a minimum amount of sodium is mandatory to sustain life, modern diets frequently contain excessive dietary salt. Modern medical studies show that chronic high sodium intake stresses the kidneys and elevates blood pressure, increasing the risk of hypertension and cardiovascular disease. Balanced sodium intake remains a cornerstone of modern preventive medicine.</p>"""

